.TH RING 2
.SH NAME
Ring, ring_push, ring_drain, ring_drain_line, ring_available \- a byte ring between processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "Ring :: struct { buf: []u8, head: u64, tail: u64 }"
.B "ring_push :: proc(r: ^Ring, b: u8)"
.B "ring_drain :: proc(r: ^Ring, out: []u8) -> int"
.B "ring_drain_line :: proc(r: ^Ring, out: []u8, stop: u8) -> int"
.B "ring_available :: proc(r: ^Ring) -> u64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B Ring
is a byte queue over a caller-supplied buffer,
.IR buf ,
and two monotonic counters.
It is what a server keeps between a byte's arrival and the read that
takes it: a thread that reads a device pushes what arrives, and the
handler drains what a client asked for.
The counters, not a length, say what is queued, so full and empty are
never confused.
.PP
Both ends are lockless.
.I Ring_push
is the producer's half: it stores the byte, then publishes the new
.I head
with a release store, so a drainer never sees a seat the byte has not
taken yet.
A full ring drops the byte; a stream nobody is serving is not a place to
park a device behind.
.I Ring_drain
is the consumer's half: it reads
.I head
once, takes what that covers into
.IR out ,
and publishes the new
.IR tail .
Both answer the number of bytes moved.
.PP
.I Ring_drain_line
is
.I ring_drain
that stops after the first
.I stop
byte, so one read answers one line however many are queued behind it.
When
.I out
fills, or the ring holds no
.I stop
byte, it answers everything there is, unterminated.
.PP
.I Ring_available
reports the bytes waiting, for a
.B Tgetattr
reply's size field.
.PP
A
.B Ring
is built before any process it is shared with forks or is given its
own thread, so every side holds the same
.IR buf .
It works with a producer and a consumer that are two threads of one proc,
or a producer in one proc and a consumer in another; the loads and
stores are volatile either way.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IR Kbdfs (4)
pushes from the key thread:
.IP
.EX
for i in 0 ..< int(n) {
	libuser.ring_push(&ring, chunk[i])
}
.EE
.PP
and a handler drains it for a held read:
.IP
.EX
got := libuser.ring_drain(&ring, buf[:room])
if got > 0 {
	reply^ = vectra9.Rread{data = buf[:got]}
}
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/ring.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR 9p (2),
.IR fid (2),
.IR fork (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
None; a full ring silently drops what does not fit, and an empty drain
answers zero.
